        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: aurīga, aurīgae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aurīga, aurīgae — \"a charioteer\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aurīga equōs celeriter agēbat. The charioteer was driving the horses fast."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine, 1st declension, though it ends in -a, like nauta. Famous charioteers grew very rich."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aurīga is explained by Lewis & Short as built on an old word for the reins and agō, \"to drive\": he who handles the reins. The details are not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
